export const body = "<p>Cover shooters are a popular sub-genre in video games, thanks to the success of titles like <em>Gears of War</em> and <em>Uncharted</em>. But if we sit down and look into video game history, we can find that taking cover isn't necessarily a modern mechanic. One of the first games built with cover in mind was <em>Battle City</em>. In this game, you control a tank, and your mission is to eliminate every other tank in the level, before they destroy your base. The cities in which your battles transpire are populated by walls made of bricks or metal. Brick walls are destructible, metal indestructible. In other words, there are two types of cover in each level.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/555bc2b284513eceaecf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/555bc2b284513eceaecf.webp\" width=\"813\" height=\"698\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/377c05ea53d0ce7f18da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/377c05ea53d0ce7f18da.webp\" width=\"603\" height=\"339\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Fast forward to 2003, <em>Kill Switch</em>, a Third-person shooter (TPS), pioneered the cover shooter genre in 3D gaming. The game was released on PS2 and Xbox, which means it was controlled with a controller, using the left analog stick for movement, saving the right analog stick for camera control. This control scheme isn't very conducive to precise aiming while moving, so the game allows players to stay behind cover, temporarily reducing the task to just aiming, and take their shots at opportune moments.</p>\n\n<p>Although <em>Kill Switch</em> wasn’t a critical success, the cover system was an influential contribution to the industry.</p>\n\n<p>Three years after the release of <em>Kill Switch</em>, <em>Gears of War</em> came out and fully realized the cover system of <em>Kill Switch</em>. The whole game was built around this mechanic:</p>\n\n<ul><li>Most enemies use guns, forcing you to take cover</li><li>Your health regenerates when you aren't taking damage</li><li>You can roll or sprint to move quicker between cover</li></ul>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a101cd52dce216d5379c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a101cd52dce216d5379c.webp\" width=\"602\" height=\"336\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/c7671a9983ca7daca51e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/c7671a9983ca7daca51e.webp\" width=\"630\" height=\"418\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>With cover being the core mechanic, <em>Gears of War</em> employed a consistent visual language for all types of cover. Although the game takes place on a fictional planet, it is very reminiscent of Earth, especially with the urban aesthetic. With this setting, the environment is allowed to feature a lot of rectangular-shaped objects, which look sturdy and reliable.</p>\n\n<p>Also inspired by<em> Kill Switch</em>, the <em>Uncharted</em> series was designed as a cover-based shooter. Unlike the first <em>Gears of War</em>, those games take place in the real world, and thus the environments have to vary frequently. With every change in the setting, the game has to find new objects to become cover because not every area has tables and walls. Sometimes, you have to hide behind a fell tree.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/16efca943eb3c05342d9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/16efca943eb3c05342d9.webp\" width=\"988\" height=\"544\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/db18dc8b5789dfc5051c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/db18dc8b5789dfc5051c.webp\" width=\"605\" height=\"325\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>To spice up the combat encounters, <em>Uncharted 4</em> sometimes combines shooting with other tangential mechanics such as climbing. Here, you can see the player participate in a gunfight while hanging on to a suspended wooden cage.</p>\n\n<p>Before we end, it's a good reminder that cover systems also appear in other genres, one of which is stealth action. For example, <em>Metal Gear Solid</em> <em>5</em> provides numerous low and tall walls for under the radar maneuvering.</p>\n\n<p><em>XCOM</em>, a turn-based strategy game, also features a cover system. When your soldiers stay behind cover, their defense will be boosted. Conversely, attacking a out-of-cover target will boost your critical chance.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/272fb29cbb297b721d1b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/272fb29cbb297b721d1b.webp\" width=\"876\" height=\"494\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3db18978f9255dec3d5d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3db18978f9255dec3d5d.webp\" width=\"859\" height=\"499\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
